/**
@page build_modes Build modes

BONet builds in one of two modes, chosen at build time. They trade runtime cost
against a verification guarantee.

@section build_modes_default Default mode

The default mode is the safe choice for the verifier: every BONet operation is
guaranteed to pass verification regardless of the program it sits in, so BONet is
never the reason a program is rejected. It adds about 10% of runtime overhead
over plain BPF.

Develop and debug in this mode.

This mode needs @c -mcpu=v3. clang 16–19 default to v1; from clang 18 on, a lower
level stops the build with an error.

@section build_modes_align Alignment

In the default mode BONet reads and writes multi-byte packet fields with single
loads and stores at offsets the verifier only knows as a range. Kernels that
enforce alignment reject them: those built without
@c CONFIG_HAVE_EFFICIENT_UNALIGNED_ACCESS (RISC-V, LoongArch and MIPS in their
default configurations, SPARC64) and any program loaded with
@c BPF_F_STRICT_ALIGNMENT.

Set @c BNT_STRICT_ALIGN=1 for them: every such access becomes byte-wise. Passing
@c -D__TARGET_ARCH_riscv, @c _loongarch, @c _mips or @c _sparc sets it
automatically. The inline and legacy modes and the TC backends do not need it.

@section build_modes_perf Performance mode

Set @c BNT_INLINE_ALL=1 to trade the guarantee for speed. It removes most of the
overhead, but verification then may depend on the surrounding program.

Once a program is stable, switching to @c BNT_INLINE_ALL is worth trying for the
lower overhead; be ready to revert if it no longer verifies.

@section build_modes_legacy Legacy kernels

Kernels older than 5.12 cannot use the default mode. Set @c BNT_LEGACY_KERNEL=1 for them: it forces @c BNT_INLINE_ALL
and adds the barriers needed to verify there. It must be set explicitly, since
the build cannot detect the target kernel.
*/
